Pairwise-comparison leaderboards such as Chatbot Arena publish Bradley--Terry scores with bootstrap confidence intervals. An interval says how far the ranking moves under resampling, not how many existing comparisons a conclusion such as ``model $a$ is above model $b$'' rests on. We measure the fewest comparisons whose deletion, or whose change of outcome, reverses a conclusion (a verified upper bound), and scale it by the conclusion's statistical separation so that datasets of different size can be compared. To judge whether a count is large, we compare it with data simulated from the dataset's own fitted model on the same graph. Across crowd votes, expert labels, LLM-judge labels and sports seasons, real data stay within a preset margin of this reference wherever enough pairs allow a decision, as a first-order identity makes largely expected; the design shows instead in how wide the bootstrap interval is relative to its model-based width. Random noise of the size of the smallest targeted change essentially never reverses a conclusion, yet separated intervals do not survive small targeted changes in large crowd and judge datasets. The comparison with the reference has limited power: in a simulated control it did not respond to overdispersion, position bias or cyclic structure of the sizes tried, while the ratio of bootstrap to model-based standard errors did. Design is confounded with dataset, so claims concern the instances studied.
